<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">San Michele Maggiore, Pavia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">San Michele Maggiore is a Romanesque basilica in Pavia, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>, rebuilt in sandstone in the early 12th century on the site of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> church. It was used for royal coronations, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> was crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> there in 1155. Its façade is covered with carved bands of animals, monsters and figures.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/san-michele-maggiore-pavia.jpg" alt="San Michele Maggiore, Pavia" width="320" height="264" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Groume · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:San_Michele_crop.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Ostrogothic silverware found at the site in 1968 suggests that an early Christian basilica may have stood there in the 5th century; the plates, spoon and cup fragment, hidden beneath a later tomb, are now in the Pavia Civic Museums. Between 662 and 671 King Grimoald had a church dedicated to St Michael built on the site of the Lombard Palace chapel. It burned in 1004, and only the lower part of its bell tower survives. Work on the present crypt, choir and transept began in the late 11th century and was finished by 1130.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The basilica, a well-preserved example of the Lombard Romanesque style, served as the model for other Pavia churches such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-pietro-in-ciel-doro">San Pietro in Ciel d'Oro</a> and San Teodoro, but differs from them in its use of sandstone instead of brick. It has a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> cross plan with a nave, two aisles and a transept 38 m long, against 55 m for the whole church, with a façade of its own. Over the crossing rises a 30 m octagonal dome on squinches, reportedly the earliest of its form in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a>. The nave vaults were rebuilt between 1488 and 1491 by Iacopo da <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraklion">Candia</a> and his son Agostino.</p><p>Inside are fragments of a pavement mosaic with the Labours of the Months, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>-clad crucifix two metres high commissioned in the 10th century by the abbess Raingarda, the high altar of 1383 holding the remains of Sts Ennodius and Eleucadius, and a Nativity carved by Baldino da Surso in 1473.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The church was the seat of royal coronations, among them those of Berengar I in 888, Arduin in 1002 and Henry II in 1004. Its two portals and monumental transept, a plan common in German imperial churches but absent elsewhere in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, served the ceremony. The procession entered by the northern portal, which originally connected the basilica to the Royal Palace, the throne was set on stones in the nave, and the procession left by the southern Porta Speciosa. In February 1397 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</a> held a ceremony there modelled on the early medieval coronations, and he ordered that his heart be kept in the basilica.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “San Michele Maggiore, Pavia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its churches, such as <b>San Michele Maggiore</b> where kings were crowned and San Pietro in Ciel d'Oro with Saint Augustine's tomb, reflected its royal past.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-pavia">Commune of Pavia</a></li></ul></section>
